1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,297
A release goes out on Friday.

2
00:00:02,297 --> 00:00:02,917
By Monday,

3
00:00:02,917 --> 00:00:04,156
the orders are gone,

4
00:00:04,156 --> 00:00:05,334
the cart is frozen,

5
00:00:05,334 --> 00:00:07,007
and the app cannot connect.

6
00:00:07,557 --> 00:00:12,698
Can you ship the database, survive all of that, and prove it? Not by luck. With evidence.

7
00:00:13,248 --> 00:00:15,707
This is the databases mastery lesson.

8
00:00:15,707 --> 00:00:16,903
One real scenario,

9
00:00:16,903 --> 00:00:19,562
every skill from the last eight lessons,

10
00:00:19,562 --> 00:00:21,888
and a judge that checks six things.

11
00:00:22,438 --> 00:00:24,806
Ship it. Break it. Recover it. Prove it.

12
00:00:26,356 --> 00:00:29,428
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

13
00:00:29,978 --> 00:00:34,074
One: a look back at the whole domain, eight objectives, in one picture.

14
00:00:34,624 --> 00:00:38,272
Two: the mastery scenario. Six missions, in a fixed order.

15
00:00:38,822 --> 00:00:43,771
Three: a live worked example of the whole scenario on a real post gress server.

16
00:00:44,321 --> 00:00:45,533
Four: your own run,

17
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:46,873
judged by six checks,

18
00:00:46,873 --> 00:00:49,679
with a report that documents what you found.

19
00:00:49,679 --> 00:00:50,572
Then clean up.

20
00:00:51,122 --> 00:00:54,749
This is lesson nine, the last lesson of the databases domain.

21
00:00:55,299 --> 00:00:57,667
Ship it. Break it. Recover it. Prove it.

22
00:00:59,717 --> 00:01:04,005
First, the map of the domain. Eight objectives, and you have done every one.

23
00:01:04,555 --> 00:01:08,950
Concepts: tables, schemas, connections, transactions, and migrations.

24
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,980
Connect and inspect: P S Q L, and simple diagnostic S Q L.

25
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:18,284
Credentials and least privilege: the app gets only what it needs.

26
00:01:18,834 --> 00:01:23,528
Configuration and storage: a server with its own folder and its own settings.

27
00:01:24,078 --> 00:01:28,451
Migrations: change the structure safely, in order, in one transaction.

28
00:01:29,001 --> 00:01:32,542
Backup and restore: and prove the restored data with numbers.

29
00:01:33,092 --> 00:01:38,127
Sessions, locks, and slow queries: look first, then end only the one that blocks.

30
00:01:38,677 --> 00:01:43,029
And troubleshooting: four layers of connection errors, and failed migrations.

31
00:01:43,579 --> 00:01:46,331
Today, all eight work together in one scenario.

32
00:01:47,881 --> 00:01:50,036
Six missions. Do them in this order.

33
00:01:50,586 --> 00:01:51,947
Mission one: deploy.

34
00:01:51,947 --> 00:01:53,239
Run the migrations.

35
00:01:53,239 --> 00:01:54,464
One of them fails,

36
00:01:54,464 --> 00:01:55,621
so find the typo,

37
00:01:55,621 --> 00:01:56,097
fix it,

38
00:01:56,097 --> 00:01:57,050
and run again.

39
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:58,907
Mission two: access.

40
00:01:58,907 --> 00:02:00,409
Create the app's login,

41
00:02:00,409 --> 00:02:01,782
with least privilege.

42
00:02:01,782 --> 00:02:03,677
It may read and write orders,

43
00:02:03,677 --> 00:02:05,179
and only read products.

44
00:02:05,179 --> 00:02:06,944
It may not delete anything.

45
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:09,377
Mission three: connect the app.

46
00:02:09,377 --> 00:02:10,471
It fails to start.

47
00:02:10,471 --> 00:02:11,444
Study the error,

48
00:02:11,444 --> 00:02:12,598
change one setting,

49
00:02:12,598 --> 00:02:13,510
and test again.

50
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:15,391
Mission four: back up.

51
00:02:15,391 --> 00:02:16,601
Before the incident,

52
00:02:16,601 --> 00:02:17,449
take a backup,

53
00:02:17,449 --> 00:02:19,264
and check that it is readable.

54
00:02:19,264 --> 00:02:19,990
Never after.

55
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,314
Mission five: the incident.

56
00:02:22,314 --> 00:02:23,826
Every order is deleted,

57
00:02:23,826 --> 00:02:26,191
and a stuck session blocks the cart.

58
00:02:26,191 --> 00:02:27,308
Find the blocker,

59
00:02:27,308 --> 00:02:28,688
write its P I D down,

60
00:02:28,688 --> 00:02:30,396
and end only that session.

61
00:02:30,946 --> 00:02:32,988
Mission six: recover and report.

62
00:02:32,988 --> 00:02:34,774
Restore into a new database,

63
00:02:34,774 --> 00:02:35,922
prove the numbers,

64
00:02:35,922 --> 00:02:37,517
and write a short report.

65
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:42,483
That report is your evidence: what you found, what failed, and what you fixed.

66
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:49,132
Go to the lab folder.

67
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:52,580
Look around.

68
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:57,418
The app folder, an empty backups folder, the server data, and the task file.

69
00:02:57,968 --> 00:03:01,382
Set up the admin shortcuts.

70
00:03:01,932 --> 00:03:05,132
Host, port, and user are now set for every command.

71
00:03:05,682 --> 00:03:13,746
Mission one: deploy.

72
00:03:14,296 --> 00:03:17,307
Zero zero one and zero zero two apply.

73
00:03:17,307 --> 00:03:23,170
Zero zero three fails and rolls back: column status status does not exist.

74
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:23,725
A typo.

75
00:03:24,275 --> 00:03:32,360
Fix the typo.

76
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:37,305
Run it again.

77
00:03:37,855 --> 00:03:40,479
All three applied. Migrations up to date.

78
00:03:41,029 --> 00:03:44,208
Go back.

79
00:03:44,758 --> 00:03:54,592
Mission two: the login.

80
00:03:55,142 --> 00:03:55,866
Create role.

81
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:58,157
The password comes from the task file.

82
00:03:58,157 --> 00:03:59,845
It is never typed into code,

83
00:03:59,845 --> 00:04:02,076
and never put in a video description.

84
00:04:02,626 --> 00:04:09,047
Grant the connection.

85
00:04:10,897 --> 00:04:17,126
Grant on tables.

86
00:04:28,976 --> 00:04:32,710
Three grants. Not one of them is delete, drop, or superuser.

87
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:39,852
Prove it.

88
00:04:43,902 --> 00:04:46,100
Permission denied for table orders.

89
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:47,921
That is exactly what we want.

90
00:04:47,921 --> 00:04:50,622
A mistake in the app cannot wipe the table.

91
00:04:51,172 --> 00:04:55,246
Mission three: start the app.

92
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:56,964
Connection refused,

93
00:04:56,964 --> 00:04:58,563
port fifty-four forty-one.

94
00:04:58,563 --> 00:04:59,608
The second layer,

95
00:04:59,608 --> 00:05:00,161
the port.

96
00:05:00,161 --> 00:05:01,514
Nothing listens there.

97
00:05:02,064 --> 00:05:10,213
Fix the port.

98
00:05:10,763 --> 00:05:15,584
Start the app again.

99
00:05:16,134 --> 00:05:19,654
App OK, eight orders. Four missions of the first half done.

100
00:05:20,204 --> 00:05:31,212
Mission four: back up.

101
00:05:31,762 --> 00:05:38,546
Is it readable?

102
00:05:39,096 --> 00:05:42,958
Both tables are inside, with their rows. The backup is readable.

103
00:05:43,508 --> 00:05:46,878
Mission five: the incident.

104
00:05:47,428 --> 00:05:51,503
Every order is deleted, and a session is now stuck, holding a lock.

105
00:05:52,053 --> 00:05:55,168
What does the app say?

106
00:05:55,718 --> 00:05:56,205
App OK,

107
00:05:56,205 --> 00:05:57,039
zero orders.

108
00:05:57,039 --> 00:05:58,500
The app is connected,

109
00:05:58,500 --> 00:05:59,961
and the data is gone.

110
00:05:59,961 --> 00:06:02,395
Connectivity was never the problem.

111
00:06:02,945 --> 00:06:09,046
Who is blocking?

112
00:06:20,896 --> 00:06:23,300
The cart API session is active,

113
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:24,076
and waits.

114
00:06:24,076 --> 00:06:26,867
Its blocked by column holds a P I D,

115
00:06:26,867 --> 00:06:29,116
and that P I D is report job,

116
00:06:29,116 --> 00:06:30,667
idle in transaction.

117
00:06:31,217 --> 00:06:41,777
Write the P I D down.

118
00:06:42,527 --> 00:06:49,482
End only that session.

119
00:06:54,432 --> 00:06:57,504
True. The session is gone, and its lock is released.

120
00:06:58,054 --> 00:07:03,131
Did the cart finish?

121
00:07:05,081 --> 00:07:07,897
Nine. The waiting cart update finished by itself.

122
00:07:08,447 --> 00:07:14,527
Mission six: recover.

123
00:07:15,077 --> 00:07:23,760
Restore into it.

124
00:07:24,310 --> 00:07:27,296
Validate.

125
00:07:31,046 --> 00:07:36,038
Eight orders. Every order is back. Only now would we copy the data into production.

126
00:07:36,588 --> 00:07:46,231
Write the report.

127
00:07:46,781 --> 00:07:50,066
Look at it.

128
00:07:50,616 --> 00:07:51,332
Four lines.

129
00:07:51,332 --> 00:07:52,372
Orders restored,

130
00:07:52,372 --> 00:07:53,152
the blocker,

131
00:07:53,152 --> 00:07:54,517
the failed migration,

132
00:07:54,517 --> 00:07:55,362
and the port.

133
00:07:55,362 --> 00:07:57,963
A teammate can read this in ten seconds,

134
00:07:57,963 --> 00:07:58,808
and trust it.

135
00:07:59,358 --> 00:08:01,726
Run the judge.

136
00:08:02,276 --> 00:08:04,127
Six checks: least privilege,

137
00:08:04,127 --> 00:08:04,855
migrations,

138
00:08:04,855 --> 00:08:05,384
the app,

139
00:08:05,384 --> 00:08:06,177
the restore,

140
00:08:06,177 --> 00:08:06,970
the blocker,

141
00:08:06,970 --> 00:08:07,962
and the report.

142
00:08:07,962 --> 00:08:08,954
Six out of six.

143
00:08:09,504 --> 00:08:11,466
Clean up.

144
00:08:12,016 --> 00:08:15,728
The server and every session are stopped, and everything is removed.

145
00:08:16,278 --> 00:08:25,281
Is it gone?

146
00:08:25,831 --> 00:08:28,348
No response. Nothing is left behind.

147
00:08:29,898 --> 00:08:33,909
Your mastery task. The same scenario, on your own machine, without me.

148
00:08:34,459 --> 00:08:37,523
Deploy the migrations and fix the one that fails.

149
00:08:37,523 --> 00:08:40,149
Create the app login with least privilege.

150
00:08:40,149 --> 00:08:41,712
Fix the app's connection.

151
00:08:42,262 --> 00:08:45,867
Back up before anything breaks, and check that the backup is readable.

152
00:08:46,417 --> 00:08:47,573
Run the incident.

153
00:08:47,573 --> 00:08:49,680
Write down the blocker's P I D,

154
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:51,176
end only that session,

155
00:08:51,176 --> 00:08:53,351
and restore into a new database.

156
00:08:53,901 --> 00:08:57,229
Write the four line report. Then lab check, and lab down.

157
00:08:59,279 --> 00:09:02,178
Here is where to find everything for the whole domain.

158
00:09:02,178 --> 00:09:03,305
Pause on this screen,

159
00:09:03,305 --> 00:09:04,271
and write it down.

160
00:09:04,821 --> 00:09:06,480
The labs live on GitHub,

161
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:07,655
at it dash waves,

162
00:09:07,655 --> 00:09:09,244
slash dev ops dash A I,

163
00:09:09,244 --> 00:09:10,765
in the folder academy,

164
00:09:10,765 --> 00:09:11,456
slash lab.

165
00:09:12,006 --> 00:09:15,866
This lesson has two files: lab one thirty-four dot S H,

166
00:09:15,866 --> 00:09:18,534
and shortcuts one thirty-four dot S H.

167
00:09:19,084 --> 00:09:19,986
Install it,

168
00:09:19,986 --> 00:09:22,857
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

169
00:09:22,857 --> 00:09:24,744
dash m seven five five,

170
00:09:24,744 --> 00:09:27,041
lab one thirty-four dot S H,

171
00:09:27,041 --> 00:09:28,272
into slash usr,

172
00:09:28,272 --> 00:09:29,257
slash local,

173
00:09:29,257 --> 00:09:30,241
slash S bin.

174
00:09:30,791 --> 00:09:35,875
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one thirty-four dot S H.

175
00:09:35,875 --> 00:09:37,296
You get three: lab,

176
00:09:37,296 --> 00:09:37,595
P G,

177
00:09:37,595 --> 00:09:38,492
and use D B.

178
00:09:39,042 --> 00:09:40,678
You need post gress Q L,

179
00:09:40,678 --> 00:09:41,904
server and client,

180
00:09:41,904 --> 00:09:44,221
and port fifty-five fourteen free.

181
00:09:44,221 --> 00:09:46,946
The lab runs its own private post gress,

182
00:09:46,946 --> 00:09:48,173
in its own folder.

183
00:09:48,723 --> 00:09:50,688
Lab up prepares the project.

184
00:09:50,688 --> 00:09:53,074
Lab incident starts the emergency.

185
00:09:53,074 --> 00:09:54,969
Lab check judges your work.

186
00:09:54,969 --> 00:09:56,653
Lab down removes it all.

187
00:09:56,653 --> 00:09:58,899
Only run it on your own machine.

188
00:09:59,449 --> 00:10:01,966
The earlier lessons each have their own lab,

189
00:10:01,966 --> 00:10:03,225
with the same pattern.

190
00:10:03,225 --> 00:10:05,742
Redo any of them whenever you need practice.

191
00:10:07,792 --> 00:10:09,861
Pass or fail. You need all six.

192
00:10:10,411 --> 00:10:13,825
One: the app login has least privilege, and cannot delete.

193
00:10:14,375 --> 00:10:16,572
Two: all three migrations are applied.

194
00:10:17,122 --> 00:10:19,554
Three: the app connects and reads its data.

195
00:10:20,104 --> 00:10:24,413
Four: a readable backup, restored into a new database, with eight orders.

196
00:10:24,963 --> 00:10:28,185
Five: the blocker is gone, and the waiting update finished.

197
00:10:28,735 --> 00:10:31,487
Six: the report has the four correct values.

198
00:10:32,037 --> 00:10:33,338
To pass, all six.

199
00:10:35,388 --> 00:10:37,159
Your turn, on your own machine.

200
00:10:37,709 --> 00:10:41,719
One: lab up, and use D B. Deploy, and fix migration three.

201
00:10:42,269 --> 00:10:46,301
Two: create the login with least privilege, and prove it cannot delete.

202
00:10:46,851 --> 00:10:49,027
Three: fix the app's port, and back up.

203
00:10:49,577 --> 00:10:50,887
Four: lab incident.

204
00:10:50,887 --> 00:10:52,059
Find the blocker,

205
00:10:52,059 --> 00:10:53,506
write the P I D down,

206
00:10:53,506 --> 00:10:55,022
end only that session,

207
00:10:55,022 --> 00:10:55,849
and restore.

208
00:10:56,399 --> 00:11:01,050
Five: write the report, then lab check until six out of six. Then lab down.

209
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:03,435
Pause the video, and begin now.

210
00:11:18,985 --> 00:11:19,860
Quick check.

211
00:11:20,410 --> 00:11:22,692
Why do you take the backup before the incident?

212
00:11:23,242 --> 00:11:25,994
Which rights did the app get, and which did it not get?

213
00:11:26,544 --> 00:11:28,336
How did you know which session to end?

214
00:11:28,886 --> 00:11:30,252
What does your report prove?

215
00:11:30,802 --> 00:11:34,141
Objectives covered: all eight of M C fourteen,

216
00:11:34,141 --> 00:11:35,738
databases for dev ops.

217
00:11:35,738 --> 00:11:38,641
Mastery evidence: a verifiable scenario,

218
00:11:38,641 --> 00:11:39,585
with results,

219
00:11:39,585 --> 00:11:40,747
troubleshooting,

220
00:11:40,747 --> 00:11:41,618
and cleanup.

221
00:11:42,168 --> 00:11:44,237
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

222
00:11:54,787 --> 00:11:58,223
Congratulations: the databases domain is complete.

223
00:11:58,223 --> 00:12:01,315
Next: software testing and quality assurance.

224
